# Definition of Cloud Computing
Cloud Computing is a technology that delivers computing services over the internet. These services include servers, storage, databases, networking, software, analytics, and intelligence.

Instead of storing data or running programs on a local computer or personal server, cloud computing allows users to access resources from remote data centers through the internet.

Simple definition:
Cloud computing means using computing resources through the internet instead of your own hardware.


2. Examples of Cloud Computing
Some common cloud-based services used in daily life are:
Google Drive – Online file storage
Gmail – Email service
Dropbox – File sharing and storage
Netflix – Online video streaming
These applications store and process data on cloud servers.


3. Characteristics of Cloud Computing(OBRRM):
On-Demand Self Service
Users can automatically access computing resources whenever needed without human interaction with the provider.

Broad Network Access 
Cloud services are available over the internet and can be accessed using devices such as laptops, smartphones, or tablets.

Resource Pooling
Cloud providers share computing resources among multiple users.

Rapid Elasticity
Resources can be quickly increased or decreased depending on the demand.

Measured Service
Users pay only for the resources they use (pay-as-you-go model).



# History of Cloud
The development of Cloud Computing happened over many years with the growth of computers, networking, and the internet.

1. 1960s – Early Idea of Cloud
The idea of cloud computing started in the 1960s.

Computer scientist John McCarthy suggested that computing could be provided like a public utility, similar to electricity or water.

At that time computers were very expensive, so organizations shared large mainframe computers.

This concept was called Time Sharing.

2. 1990s – Internet and Virtualization
During the 1990s, the internet became widely available.

Companies started providing virtual private networks (VPN) and remote computing services.

The term “Cloud” began to be used to represent the internet in network diagrams.

This period helped create the foundation for modern cloud computing.


3. 2000–2006 – Beginning of Modern Cloud
In the early 2000s, companies started building large data centers.
A major milestone happened in 2006 when Amazon launched Amazon Web Services (AWS).

AWS allowed users to rent:
Virtual servers
Storage
Networking services

This introduced the pay-as-you-go cloud model.

4. 2008 – Growth of Cloud Platforms
Other technology companies also launched cloud platforms.

Google launched Google App Engine
Microsoft introduced Microsoft Azure

These platforms allowed developers to build and deploy applications on the cloud.

5. 2010 – Rapid Expansion
Around 2010, cloud computing started growing very fast.

Organizations began using cloud for:
Web applications
Big data processing
Mobile applications
Online storage

Cloud providers also introduced services like virtual machines, databases, and serverless computing.


6. Present Day – Advanced Cloud Era
Today cloud computing is used in many advanced technologies such as:

Artificial Intelligence (AI)
Machine Learning
Internet of Things (IoT)
Big Data Analytics
Streaming services
Online gaming
Major cloud providers today include:
Amazon Web Services
Microsoft Azure
Google Cloud

✅ Short Summary:
1960s → Idea of shared computers
1990s → Internet growth
2006 → AWS launched cloud services
Today → Cloud used worldwide for many applications.

# Q why do we actually need cloud.
We need Cloud Computing because it makes computing easier, cheaper, and more flexible than traditional systems.

1. Cost Saving

Organizations do not need to buy expensive servers, hardware, and infrastructure.
They can use cloud services and pay only for what they use.

2. Scalability
Cloud allows users to increase or decrease resources (storage, servers, etc.) according to their needs.

Example:
If a website gets more visitors, the cloud can increase server capacity automatically.

3. Access from Anywhere
Cloud services can be accessed from anywhere using the internet.

Example:
Files stored in Google Drive can be accessed from any device.

4. Data Backup and Recovery
Cloud providers automatically backup data, which helps in recovering data if a system fails.

5. High Performance
Cloud providers use powerful data centers and advanced hardware, which gives better speed and performance.

6. Easy Collaboration
Multiple users can work on the same file or project at the same time.

Example:
Using Google Docs, many people can edit the same document together.

7. Automatic Updates
Cloud providers automatically update software and security, so users do not need to manage it.

# Evolution of computing

Evolution of Computing ka simple matlab hai computer ka time ke saath development hona.
Simple Steps

🔹 1. Mainframe (1960–70s)
बड़ी-बड़ी कंप्यूटर मशीनें
सिर्फ बड़ी कंपनियां इस्तेमाल करती थीं
👉 Example: IBM

🔹 2. Minicomputer (1980–1985)
Mainframe से छोटे और सस्ते
👉 Example: DEC

🔹 3. PCs (1985–1990)
Personal Computer आया (घर/ऑफिस में use)
👉 Example: Intel

🔹 4. Client-Server (1990–1995)
Server data रखता है, client (user) access करता है
👉 Example: Microsoft

🔹 5. IP Networks (1995–2000)
Internet का विकास
👉 Example: Cisco

🔹 6. Mobile Devices (2001–2008)
Mobile और wireless technology आई
👉 Example: Nokia

🔹 7. Cloud Computing (Present) ☁️
Data और software internet पर store
कहीं से भी access
👉 Example:
Amazon
Google

> Evolution of Computing ko simple way me 3 types me samjhaya jata hai:

1️⃣ Traditional IT
Sab kuch company ke own computers aur servers par hota tha.

Hardware, software, storage sab company ko khud kharidna aur maintain karna padta tha.
Cost zyada hoti thi.

Example:
Office me apna server room aur computers.

2️⃣ Virtualization
Ek physical server ko virtual machines (VM) me divide kar diya jata hai.

Ek hi server par multiple operating systems aur applications run ho sakte hain.
Hardware ka better use hota hai.
Example:
VMware, VirtualBox.

3️⃣ Cloud Computing
Data aur applications internet ke through remote servers par store hote hain.

User ko hardware kharidne ki zarurat nahi hoti.
Pay-as-you-use model hota hai.
Example:
AWS, Google Cloud, Microsoft Azure.


# Characteristics of cc
Cloud computing has some key features that make it powerful and widely used.

🔹 1. On-Demand Self-Service ⚙️
User can create or use resources anytime without human help
No need to contact service provider
👉 Example: Launch a server instantly on Amazon Web Services

🔹 2. Broad Network Access 🌐
Services available over internet
Access from mobile, laptop, tablet
👉 Example: Open files in Google Drive anywhere

🔹 3. Resource Pooling 🧩
Resources shared among multiple users
Uses multi-tenant model
👉 Many users share same infrastructure securely

🔹 4. Rapid Elasticity 📈
Resources can increase/decrease quickly
Handles sudden traffic
👉 Example: E-commerce sites during sales

🔹 5. Measured Service 💰
Pay only for what you use
Usage is monitored and billed
👉 Like electricity bill system

🔹 6. High Availability & Reliability 🔄
Services available most of the time
Backup systems prevent failure
👉 Data stored in multiple locations


